// E2E test for the `:remove-class <Class>` and `:flush-destructive
// [<selector>]` REPL meta-commands (ADR 0113 Phase 4, BT-3210).
//
// These CLI-side aliases desugar to the Beamtalk expressions:
//   :remove-class <Class>          → <Class> removeFromSystem
//   :flush-destructive             → Workspace flushIncludingDestructive
//   :flush-destructive <selector>  → Workspace flush: <selector> confirmDestructive: true
//
// IMPORTANT: once `:remove-class <Class>` has run, scope a following
// `:flush-destructive` by `#Class` (a Symbol), not the bare class name —
// `removeFromSystem` already unbound the name, so a bare identifier fails to
// evaluate before the `flush:` send even happens. See the inline comment
// below where this matters.
//
// The test verifies the aliases dispatch through to the underlying
// `Behaviour>>removeFromSystem` / `WorkspaceInterface>>flushIncludingDestructive`
// / `WorkspaceInterface>>flush:confirmDestructive:` primitives. Primitive
// semantics (ChangeLog logging, staged-delete atomicity, tier
// classification) are covered separately: `remove_from_system.btscript`
// (BT-3206) and `workspace_flush_destructive.btscript` (BT-3207).
//
// IMPORTANT: meta-commands are dispatched by the REPL/test harness only when
// the entire line is `:remove-class <Class>` / `:flush-destructive` /
// `:flush-destructive <selector>` — see repl_flush_changes_dirty.btscript for
// the same caveat about composability. The interactive `y/N` terminal
// confirmation the real REPL prompts with is not exercised here — the
// protocol test harness talks to the workspace directly, bypassing the
// terminal, the same way it already does for `:remove-method`/`:flush`.
//
// A unique inline class name (rather than a shared fixture) avoids
// interfering with other REPL-protocol cases, which share one long-lived
// workspace session. Like remove_from_system.btscript, this class is never
// backed by an on-disk file (no active project context in the shared e2e
// workspace), so its `remove-class` entry is `flushable: false` — a safe
// no-op to flush, not a real file delete.

Value subclass: ReplRemoveClassDemo
  greet => "hi"
// => _

ReplRemoveClassDemo new greet
// => hi

Object allSubclasses includes: ReplRemoveClassDemo
// => true

// Save a reference before removal (`allSubclasses includes:` after removal
// needs something to compare against — the name itself no longer resolves).
replRemoveClassDemoRef := ReplRemoveClassDemo
// => _

// ===========================================================================
// :remove-class — alias for `<Class> removeFromSystem`
// ===========================================================================

// :remove-class removes the class from memory (wildcard: meta-commands
// aren't composable, so the harness can only verify the call doesn't raise
// an error — same pattern as :remove-method/:flush).
:remove-class ReplRemoveClassDemo
// => _

Object allSubclasses includes: replRemoveClassDemoRef
// => false

// removeFromSystem logged a durable `remove-class` ChangeLog entry
// (ADR 0113 Phase 1, BT-3206) — filtered by className because REPL-protocol
// cases share one long-lived ChangeLog (same convention as
// remove_from_system.btscript).
_replRemoveClassEntry := (Workspace changes select: [:e | e isRemoveClass and: [e className =:= #ReplRemoveClassDemo]]) last
// => _

_replRemoveClassEntry isRemoveClass
// => true

// Dynamically-defined (no on-disk source file) → not flushable.
_replRemoveClassEntry isFlushable
// => false

// ===========================================================================
// :flush-destructive <selector> — alias for
// `Workspace flush: <selector> confirmDestructive: true`
// ===========================================================================

// Scoped to the class above by *Symbol*, not bare identifier: `removeFromSystem`
// already unbound the `ReplRemoveClassDemo` name (see `allSubclasses includes:`
// above — the bare identifier no longer resolves), so `:flush-destructive
// ReplRemoveClassDemo` would fail to evaluate its argument expression before
// the send even happens. `#ReplRemoveClassDemo` is a Symbol *literal* — no
// resolution needed — and `Workspace flush:`'s filter normalisation
// (`beamtalk_workspace_flush:normalise_filter/1`) matches it against the
// ChangeLog entry's recorded `class` field by name, exactly the mechanism a
// destructive flush of an already-removed class needs. Not flushable, so
// this is a safe no-op — it must not raise, and must not resurrect or
// otherwise error on the entry.
:flush-destructive #ReplRemoveClassDemo
// => _

// ===========================================================================
// :flush-destructive — alias for `Workspace flushIncludingDestructive`
// ===========================================================================

// The unscoped destructive tier reaches every pending entry in the shared
// workspace ChangeLog. It must not raise even when nothing new is pending
// for this specific class.
:flush-destructive
// => _

// After all the targeted flushes, the ChangeLog is still queryable.
:changes
// => _
